class Solution {
  public int[] cableFamiliesProofStrip(int[][] links, int k) {
    int[][] a=links; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int n=a.length;int[]parent=new int[n],size=new int[n];for(int v=0;v<n;v++){parent[v]=v;size[v]=1;}class DSU {int find(int x){while(parent[x]!=x){parent[x]=parent[parent[x]];x=parent[x];}return x;}}DSU d=new DSU();for(int u=0;u<n;u++)for(int v=u+1;v<n;v++)if(a[u][v]!=0){int x=d.find(u),y=d.find(v);if(x!=y){if(size[x]<size[y]){int t=x;x=y;y=t;}parent[y]=x;size[x]+=size[y];}}for(int v=0;v<n;v++)out.add(size[d.find(v)]);}
    return out.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray();
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
The working idea

links is a symmetric 0/1 adjacency matrix. Union every connected pair, then produce the size of the component containing each vertex, in vertex order. Self-links do not change a component.

Return the entire derived sequence, preserving its order.

Why this works

Each derived value follows the unit's stated order and includes exactly the items allowed by its definition. The receipt then folds or filters those values without changing their meaning. Check the empty, singleton, repeated and maximum-size inputs before trusting the final total.

Target time O(V^2 alpha(V)); space O(n).

